Neverputt for generated9

Native C port of the Neverball 1.6.0 minigolf game. See UPSTREAM,
LICENSE.md and the preserved notices for attribution and licensing.

The platform code uses libdraw/libevent for a rio window and input,
libttf for text, the system PNG/JPEG readers for images, and /dev/audio
for a small PCM mixer. The original game, physics and level formats are
preserved. Graphics use the classic GL subset in libtinygl and VirGL.
There is no SDL, C++, Mesa or foreign compiler in the guest.

Prepare data on the host:

    ./boot/getneverputt

Then inside generated9:

    cd /sys/src/libgpu
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/libtinygl
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/games/neverputt
    mk install
    mk data
    games/neverputt

Data installation verifies the pinned original archive and keeps its
asset licenses. mk data compiles maps with the native mapc and decodes
Ogg tracks with audio/oggdec. It skips existing generated files; remove
an affected .sol or .pcm file to rebuild it after changing its source.
The build needs no game data; playing does.

Boot the current pc64 kernel with a VirGL device as described in boot/GPU.md.
Its 256 resource slots let menus keep their small textures resident; the
64 MiB backing budget is unchanged. TinyGL evicts cached GPU texture copies
if either limit is reached, retaining their contents in guest memory. For
sound, also provide a supported sound device, for example QEMU intel-hda
with hda-duplex and a host audio backend. /dev/audio uses 44.1 kHz stereo,
signed 16-bit little-endian PCM. The game reports when audio is unavailable.

See neverputt(1) for controls and command-line options. Native options
omit display modes owned by rio and unsupported graphics features.
Translations, gamepads, HMD, reflections and multisampling are not ported.

The source archive references a missing mtrl/dirt-tan image in Spinaround.
mapc reports missing images and uses unit texture coordinates; the original
material loader already supplies its default material when one is absent.
The native compiler also clamps rounded normal dot products before acos,
so valid near-parallel normals stay inside its mathematical domain.

Development validation used an amd64 QEMU/KVM guest with two CPUs, 3 GiB
RAM and VirGL. All original Neverputt maps and PCM assets were prepared
natively. Interactive play covered a completed first hole, scoring and the
second hole, keyboard and mouse input, pause, resizing and clean exit.
A session of 11,304 frames averaged 61.2 fps at 800 by 600, including
readback and window presentation. An 18-hole, four-player score screen
test averaged 27.3 fps over 180 frames, including initial texture uploads.
These are measurements of this development VM, not hardware guarantees.
The GPU device, teapot and TinyGL pixel/lifetime tests passed, including
texture eviction beyond the resource count and backing-memory limits.

The mixer has a separate regression test, requiring /dev/audio but no assets:

    cd /sys/src/games/neverputt/test
    mk test

It checks shutdown with another child process, repeated initialization and
cleanup, and transitions to holes without background music. The mixer joins
its own process without consuming the application's wait messages.
